class_name Notes
extends RefCounted
## Lore documents found around the museum. Index = note id (1-based in game).
## style: "type" (typewriter), "hand" (handwriting), "print" (book/newspaper)

const LIST := [
	{"title": "Memo: Welcome Aboard", "style": "type", "text": "TO: New Night Guard\nFROM: Brenda Holloway, Head of Night Security\n\nWelcome to the Pemberton Museum of Questionable History!\n\nYour predecessor, Gary, left us suddenly last month. No forwarding address. His coffee mug is still in the office. Please do not use it. It is evidence.\n\nThe rules on the poster are not jokes. I know they look like jokes. They were written by someone with a sense of humour, which is how you know they are serious.\n\nI will be on the radio all night. I am always on the radio.\n\n- B.H."},
	{"title": "Pemberton's Diary, p. 1", "style": "print", "text": "14th March, 1928.\n\nAcquired today, at no small expense, the Golden Duck of Ur-Quack. The dealer wept with relief as I carried it out. Odd fellow.\n\nThe Duck is magnificent. Its eyes follow you about the room. I assume this is a trick of the light. The light in this room is very poor.\n\nPlaced it in the Great Hall. Tomorrow I shall build it a pedestal worthy of its station."},
	{"title": "Gary's Patrol Notes #1", "style": "hand", "text": "Week 1.\n\nThe armour in the lobby moved again. Brenda says this is 'normal'. Brenda says a lot of things are normal.\n\nTip for the next guy (if there is one): don't blink near Sir Clanksworth. I mean it.\n\nAlso the vending machine eats quarters. That one is not supernatural. It's just evil."},
	{"title": "Pemberton's Diary, p. 2", "style": "print", "text": "2nd June, 1928.\n\nThe clockwork boy - I have named him Wolfgang - played his little tune all night without being wound. When I came down in the morning he was facing the door.\n\nThe Egyptian gentleman's bandages have been rewrapped. Neatly. By whom?\n\nThe Duck sat on its pedestal looking extremely pleased with itself."},
	{"title": "Gary's Patrol Notes #2", "style": "hand", "text": "Week 3.\n\nWOLFGANG. KEEP HIM WOUND. When the music slows down you have about thirty seconds. When the music STOPS you are already in trouble.\n\nHe's small but he's fast and he can hear you breathing. Get in a locker. Hold your breath. Think about something nice.\n\nI thought about pancakes. It worked."},
	{"title": "Formal Complaint (Egyptian Hall)", "style": "hand", "text": "To Whom It May Concern,\n\nI am writing to complain, for the 4,000th year running, about the noise.\n\nThe new night guard RUNS. Through MY hall. At NIGHT. Some of us are trying to be dead.\n\nI have also not received my tea. I was promised tea in 1928.\n\nIf this continues I will be forced to get up. Nobody wants that. I do not want that. My knees are 4,000 years old.\n\nYours, with ancient and deeply wrapped disappointment,\nThe Pharaoh's Neighbour"},
	{"title": "Gerald: Therapy Notes", "style": "type", "text": "PATIENT: Gerald (Tyrannosaurus rex, juvenile, deceased)\nSESSION 12\n\nPatient reports severe anxiety when bright lights are pointed at his face. Patient roars when startled. Patient apologises afterwards, but nobody can hear him over the roaring.\n\nPatient asked if he could have a blanket. Patient is a skeleton. Request denied on medical grounds. Patient cried. Patient does not have tear ducts. It was very confusing for everyone.\n\nRECOMMENDATION: No flash photography. Ever."},
	{"title": "Acquisition Record: The Tea Party", "style": "type", "text": "ITEM: The Tea Party (five porcelain dolls, table, six cups)\nACQUIRED: 1902, from the Ashworth estate\n\nNOTES: The Ashworth children were never found. The dolls were found at the table, with one extra cup.\n\nThe cup is always warm.\n\nStaff are reminded that the dolls do not move. If a doll appears to have moved, the staff member is reminded that they did not see it move, because they were not looking, which is the point."},
	{"title": "Letter in the Director's Desk", "style": "hand", "text": "Dear Mum,\n\nI became Director today. Sixty years after you didn't come home. They gave me your old office key by mistake. I didn't correct them.\n\nI turn on your radio every night. Sometimes I think I hear you on the security channel, telling a new guard to clock in.\n\nI've hired another guard. He's the forty-first. He seems kind. He seems a bit daft. I think he might be the one.\n\nYour Lucy (68 years old, still six on the inside)"},
	{"title": "Gary's Last Note", "style": "hand", "text": "If you're reading this, you found my locker. Sorry about the smell. That's the wax.\n\nI can't leave anymore. I tried. My feet just walk the patrol route. I still do my rounds, every night. It's the only thing that feels normal.\n\nIf you see me - and you will - DON'T let me see you. I won't mean it. But I'll still do it.\n\nThe vault keycard is in here. Brenda knows what it's for. Ask her what happened in 1963.\n\n- Gary (Night Guard, still technically employed)"},
	{"title": "Wax Hall Restoration Log", "style": "type", "text": "WAX HALL - RESTORATION LOG\n\nFigure #1: Napoleon's Neighbour - nose reattached.\nFigure #2: A Pirate (Allegedly) - hat found in the Egyptian Hall. Again.\nFigure #3: Inventor of the Spoon - fine.\nFigure #4: Brenda Holloway - figure is warm to the touch. Radio in hand will not switch off. Do not attempt to remove radio.\nFigure #5: (pending) - Night Guard, G. Finch. Not yet installed. Appears to install himself."},
	{"title": "Pemberton's Diary, p. 3", "style": "print", "text": "31st October, 1931.\n\nI understand it now. The Duck does not merely sit in the museum. The Duck OWNS the museum, and everything in it. Every exhibit it owns, it wakes.\n\nAnd anyone who stays too long among its things becomes one of its things.\n\nThere is only one remedy. In the vault beneath the Egyptian Hall is the altar where it was first awakened. Melt it there and it sleeps forever.\n\nI could not bring myself to do it. It looked at me. I have locked the vault. I am going down there now to look after it. Just for tonight."},
	{"title": "Star Theatre Booking Log", "style": "type", "text": "PEMBERTON STAR THEATRE - NIGHT SHOWS\n\n11 PM - 0 tickets\n12 AM - 0 tickets\n1 AM - 0 tickets\n2 AM - 0 tickets\n3 AM - 1 ticket. Seat 13.\n\nSeat 13 was removed in 1964.\n\nThe projector keeps starting itself at 3 AM. If you hear the music, please do not sit down. Please do not look at Seat 13. It isn't there. That's the problem."},
	{"title": "A Poem (pinned to Mr. Snuggles)", "style": "hand", "text": "To the Duchess, from Sir C.\n\nThy frame is gilt, thine eyes are cold,\nThou hast been disappointed for two hundred years untold.\nI am but empty armour, hollow as a drum,\nYet when thou watchest me, my rivets go numb.\n\nMeet me at the Grand Staircase at 4 AM.\nCome alone. Do not bring the mummy. He talks.\n\n(Please return this note to the bear if found. The bear is the postman.)"},
	{"title": "Restoration Memo #47", "style": "type", "text": "WORKSHOP - INTERNAL\n\nDo NOT remove the sheet from item #47.\n\nItem #47 is not a statue.\nItem #47 was a security guard (1987-1987).\nItem #47 still checks the windows every night.\n\nIf item #47 asks you whether the windows are locked, say YES, and keep walking. Do not tell him it has been thirty-six years."},
	{"title": "The Pemberton Gazette, 1963", "style": "print", "text": "THE PEMBERTON GAZETTE - 12 November 1963\n\nNIGHT WATCHWOMAN VANISHES FROM LOCAL MUSEUM\n\nMrs. Brenda Holloway (41), head of night security, failed to return home after her shift on Tuesday. Her radio was found switched on. Her six-year-old daughter, Lucy, waited at the window until dawn.\n\nThe curator stated that 'nothing is missing, apart from Mrs. Holloway, and possibly a spoon.'\n\nThe museum will open as usual on Thursday. A new wax figure is planned for the east wing."},
	{"title": "Brenda's Note (Unsent)", "style": "hand", "text": "New guy,\n\nIf you've come this far, you deserve the truth. I've been on this radio for sixty years. I can't remember the last time I went home. I can remember home, though. A yellow kitchen. A little girl at the window.\n\nThe Duck keeps us. All of us. Gary. The knight. The Duchess. Me.\n\nTake it to the altar. Melt it. When it sleeps, we sleep. And that's alright. It's been a very long shift.\n\nIf you ever meet a woman called Lucy... tell her Mummy finally clocked out.\n\n- B."},
]


static func title(id: int) -> String:
	return LIST[id - 1]["title"]


static func text(id: int) -> String:
	return LIST[id - 1]["text"]


static func style(id: int) -> String:
	return LIST[id - 1].get("style", "type")
